  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Cap-d'Ail, a small town squeezed between Monaco and Nice, has a pebble beach at the Pointe des Douaniers that the town hall calls "the dog beach", because dogs are tolerated there. A setting typical of this stretch of the Riviera, with views over the sea and the surrounding rocks.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The town hall (cap-dail.fr, "Bord de mer" page) writes that this beach is "also called the dog beach because dogs are tolerated there". It places the beach west of the point, on the coastal path, and advises parking at the Cap Fleuri car park, uphill and a few minutes' walk away. The Nice Côte d'Azur tourist office, repeated by the regional tourist board, gives the address "east of the cape" (6000 avenue des Douaniers) and states that dogs must be kept on a leash on the path leading to the cove, then may run free and swim once they reach it. The two sources therefore disagree on the exact orientation: look for the signs on-site.</p>
      <p>Neither the town hall nor the tourist office gives opening periods or hours for this beach, and no seasonal rule appears on the pages read; the plages.tv directory describes a small pebble beach only a few metres wide, so space is limited on busy days. Bring fresh water for your dog and pick up after it.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the coastal path, open to dogs on a leash according to the town hall, is a lovely walk before or after the swim.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The town hall states that the coastal promenade, closed to two-wheelers, is shut by municipal staff in strong wind or rough seas, and that dogs must be on a leash there; the Cap Fleuri car park is 200 metres from the promenade. The plages.tv directory also lists dog-friendly beaches nearby in Roquebrune-Cap-Martin, Menton and Villefranche-sur-Mer, whose rules should be checked with each town.</p>
    </div>

